GitHub Actions runner on Fedora
===============================

This package builds the runner from source against the .NET and Node.js that
Fedora ships, rather than unpacking the binary release GitHub publishes. It
differs from that release in a few ways:

* It runs on every architecture Fedora builds .NET for, including ppc64le
  and s390x, which GitHub publishes no runner for.
* The .NET runtime comes from dotnet-runtime-10.0, so it is updated with the
  rest of the system instead of being bundled.
* externals/node20 and externals/node24, the Node.js runtimes JavaScript
  actions run on, are symlinks to Fedora's nodejs22 and nodejs24. Fedora has
  no Node.js 20, so actions declaring "using: node20" run on Node.js 22.

Registering the runner
----------------------

Get a registration token from the repository or organisation you want the
runner to join (Settings -> Actions -> Runners -> New self-hosted runner),
then:

    sudo github-actions-runner-config --unattended --disableupdate \
        --url https://github.com/OWNER/REPO --token TOKEN

--disableupdate matters: without it the runner tries to replace itself with a
binary release from GitHub. There is none for ppc64le or s390x, and where
there is one it would be unpacked over files this package owns, which belong
to root, so the update fails either way. Update the runner with dnf instead.

Running it
----------

    sudo systemctl enable --now github-actions-runner
    journalctl -u github-actions-runner -f

The service does not start until the runner is registered.

To unregister, stop the service and run:

    sudo github-actions-runner-config remove --token TOKEN

State
-----

Registration (.runner, .credentials), the job work folder (_work) and the
runner's own logs (_diag) are kept in /var/lib/github-actions-runner, which
belongs to the github-actions-runner user. The binaries in that directory
belong to root, so a job cannot rewrite them.

Only one runner is configured per machine this way. For more, follow
upstream's instructions and give each one its own directory.

Jobs run as the github-actions-runner user with that user's privileges, on the
machine itself, so only use this with workflows you trust. Container and
service-container steps additionally need podman-docker or docker.
